# GCSL

_GCSL / Getting Started_

> A language for typed configuration values and executable scripts.

GCSL is a statically typed scripting language. A program can describe a
configuration record, compute a value, or run commands. You use the same
expressions for all three, with function types separating pure computation
from operations that can change the outside world.

The `gcsl` command runs programs, checks them without execution, formats
source files, and serves editor requests. Starting it without a command
opens an interactive read-evaluate-print loop (REPL).

## Values first

A file is a block of declarations and expressions. Named fields form its
resulting record. This configuration computes one field from another:

```gcsl
name = "gcsl"
version = "0.1.0"
archive = "{name}-{version}.tar.gz"
```

A file can instead return one expression. Use `let` for private values that
don't become fields of a record. The checker catches incompatible types and
unresolved names before any part of the program runs.

## Execution and trust

Pure functions use `:` before their result type. Impure functions use
`->`; they can run processes, access files, and await deferred work.
Memoized functions use `->>` and reuse results within an execution context.
A `lazy` expression delays work until it is awaited. It doesn't start a
background task.

GCSL's type system is not a sandbox. Running a script gives its process and
file operations the permissions of the process running GCSL. Check an
unfamiliar program with `gcsl check` before deciding whether to run it.
Checking does not establish that executing it is safe.

## Where to go next

Start with [Running a First Program](/gcsl/getting-started/running-a-first-program.md), then
read [Values and Bindings](/gcsl/language/values-and-bindings.md). Use `gcsl(1)` for the
command interface, `gcsl(5)` for language rules, and `gcsl-stdlib(3)` for
standard functions.

Pekit embeds GCSL to provide its own build interface. Its definitions and
commands belong to Pekit. The separate `tree-sitter-gcsl` repository owns
its editor grammar and queries; the compiler parser in this repository is
the authority for whether a GCSL program is accepted.

---

# Running a First Program

_GCSL / Getting Started_

> Create, check, and run a small GCSL file.

You can run a single GCSL file without creating a workspace. This example
checks a small configuration and prints its computed description.

## Before you start

You need the following:

- The `gcsl` executable on your command search path.
- A writable directory for the example file.

## Create and run the file

1. Save the following text as `hello.gcsl`:

   ```gcsl
   let component = {name = "gcsl"; version = "0.1.0"}
   "{component.name} {component.version}"
   ```

2. Check the file without running it:

   ```sh
   gcsl check hello.gcsl
   ```

3. Run the file:

   ```sh
   gcsl run hello.gcsl
   ```

## Check the result

The check reports `no problems in 1 file checked` on standard error. The
run prints the string contents on standard output:

```text
gcsl 0.1.0
```

A file whose result is `{}`, the empty record, prints nothing. Diagnostics
go to standard error. See `gcsl(1)` for exit statuses and output modes.

## Trying expressions interactively

Run `gcsl` without arguments to open the REPL. Enter `let n = 21`, then
`n * 2` on the next line. The second line prints `42 : Int`. Each input is
one line; separate several items on that line with semicolons. Press
**Ctrl+D** to leave the session.

---

# Values and Bindings

_GCSL / The Language_

> Understand block results, records, local names, and value updates.

GCSL programs compute values. A block can return one value or construct a
record of named fields, so you can use configuration data inside ordinary
expressions without a separate data format.

## Blocks and records

Newlines or semicolons separate items inside a file or `{ ... }` block.
A block without fields returns its final expression, or an explicit
`= expression` result. A block with fields returns their record instead;
it cannot also have an explicit result. The empty record `{}` represents
an operation with no useful result.

A field is a public part of the resulting record. A `let` binding is local
to its scope. This block keeps the intermediate name private:

```gcsl
let release = "0.1.0"
name = "gcsl"
archive = "{name}-{release}.tar.gz"
```

Top-level fields and named functions can refer to declarations written
later. Inner blocks evaluate their ordinary fields in order. Private
`let` bindings become available after their declaration.

## Updating a binding

`:=` replaces the value associated with a local name. For an ordinary
`let`, this is restricted to the block that declares it. Use `let mut`
when an inner block of the same function needs to update the variable:

```gcsl
let mut total = 0
for cost in [3, 8, 5] {
    total := total + cost
}
total
```

The variable keeps its declared or inferred type. Values themselves do not
change: replacing a record field or list element constructs an updated
value for the binding. Other bindings and previously created closures
keep the values they already held.

A closure captures values when it is created. It cannot update a mutable
variable belonging to its enclosing function. Pass in a value and return
an updated value when you need that communication.

## Names and arithmetic

Names can contain hyphens. Write spaces around subtraction so that
`left - right` is an operation rather than a name. Integer arithmetic uses
signed 64-bit values with checked overflow. Floating-point values use
64-bit precision. `/` produces a floating-point result; `//` and `%` are
integer floor division and modulo.

Integer literals adapt to an expected numeric type. A bound `Int` does
not silently become a `Float`; use `float(value)` when that conversion is
intended. Conditions require `Bool`, not a nonzero integer or a nonempty
string. See `gcsl(5)` for syntax and operator rules.

## Durations and sizes

`Duration` and `Size` hold amounts with units. Write a duration as a
number followed by units from largest to smallest: `30s`, `500ms`,
`1h30m`, `2d`. The units are `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, `ms`, `us` and `ns`, and a
day is exactly 24 hours. Write a size with one unit: `512B`, the decimal
`KB` to `PB`, or the binary `KiB` to `PiB`, as in `2GiB`. The last part of
a literal can have a fraction if the result is a whole number of
nanoseconds or bytes, so `1.5s` and `0.5KiB` are allowed but `1.5ns` is
not. Units are case-sensitive.

```gcsl
let timeout = 1m30s
retry_after = timeout // 3
budget = 2 * timeout
memory = 2GiB + 512MiB
long = timeout > 1m
message = "giving up after {timeout}"
```

Add or subtract two values of the same kind, multiply one by an `Int`,
and use `//` to divide by an `Int` or by another value of the same kind.
Dividing by the same kind gives an `Int`. `%` gives the remainder of one
value by another of the same kind. Values of the same kind can be
compared. Durations and sizes never mix with each other, with `Int` or
with `Float`, and an integer is never a duration, so write `0s` rather
than `0`. `/` is not available, because the result would not be a whole
number of the smallest unit.

A value is shown as its simplest literal: `retry_after` above is `30s`,
`budget` is `3m`, and `memory` is `2560MiB`.

---

# Records and Types

_GCSL / The Language_

> Compose structural records and apply annotations and defaults.

A record type describes the fields a value supplies. GCSL uses structural
types: two declarations with the same structure describe the same type,
and a record can be used where fewer of its fields are required.

## Declaring a shape

Use `type` to give a structure a reusable name. An annotation checks a
value against that structure. Defaults supply omitted fields in a record
literal checked against the type:

```gcsl
type Install = {from: String, exclude: List<String> = []}
let item: Install = {from = "src"}
item.exclude
```

Defaults are constant expressions, not arbitrary function calls. They are
applied where a record literal has the expected type; they don't add
fields to an already constructed variable with a smaller type.

An annotation also determines what fields later code can see. Extra
fields in a record literal produce a warning when an expected type hides
them. A structural alias does not introduce a distinct runtime identity.

## Generic types

A type can take type parameters, named in angle brackets after its name.
Each use names the types to put in their place:

```gcsl
type Pair<A, B> = {first: A, second: B}
let entry: Pair<String, Int> = {first = "port"; second = 8080}
entry.second
```

A use must give as many type arguments as the type has parameters, and a
type without parameters takes none. Because types are structural,
`Pair<String, Int>` is the same type as `{first: String, second: Int}`. A
generic type can be built from another, as in
`type Named<T> = Pair<String, T>`. It can't refer to itself, and it can't
spread one of its parameters, because the parameter might not be a record.

## Enums

An extensible enum is a type whose variants any module can add. Declare
the enum, with an optional bound that every variant's payload must fit,
and then its variants, each with a payload type:

```gcsl
enum Shape extensible<{*: Int}>
enum Shape::Square<{side: Int}>
enum Shape::Point
square = Shape::Square({side = 3})
```

A variant is also a value: `Shape::Square` on its own has the type
`Variant<Shape, {side: Int}>`. Calling it makes a value of the enum,
and you can pass it to a function as a key. `kind.matches(value)` tests
whether a value is of that variant, and `kind.payload(value)` takes out
its payload:

```gcsl
enum Tool extensible
enum Tool::C<{cc: String}>
fn find<T>(kind: Variant<Tool, T>, tools: List<Tool>, fallback: T): T {
    for t in tools { if kind.matches(t) { return kind.payload(t) } }
    fallback
}
cc = find(Tool::C, [Tool::C({cc = "gcc"})], {cc = "cc"}).cc
```

Unlike records, enums are nominal: two enums are different types even
if they look alike. `value is Tool` tests whether a value is of the enum.
Values of an enum are compared with `==` only when the enum has a bound,
because an unbounded payload might hold a function. A variant is visible
in its own file, to the other files of its directory and the directories
below it, and to every file that imports its module.

## Partial records

`Partial<T>` holds any of the fields of the record type `T`. Use it to
describe a record by only some of its fields, and `.matches()` to test
a whole record against it:

```gcsl
type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
let wanted: Partial<Platform> = {arch = "x86_64"}
fits = wanted.matches({arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"})
```

A field of a partial record can't be read by name, because it may be
absent.

## Records of any fields

A rest-field, `*: T`, gives the type of every field that a record type
doesn't name. Use it when the field names are data, such as paths. A
`{*: String}` holds any fields, each a `String`. Named fields keep their
own types, so `{to: String, *: Int}` holds a `String` field `to`, and any
other fields are `Int`s:

```gcsl
let files: {*: String} = {"bin/*" = "bin/*"; "sbin/*" = "sbin/*"}
files
```

Write the record literal where the type is expected: in an annotation,
an argument or a definition's result. There, each field that the type
doesn't name is checked against the rest-field. A value whose own type
has no rest-field doesn't fit one, because a record keeps fields that its
static type hides, and they could be of any type.

Read a named field as usual. A field that only the rest-field covers
can't be read by name, and a record whose type has a rest-field can't be
spread. Typed equality compares every field, and `is` and `as` test each
field that the rest-field covers.

## Combining fields

Dotted definitions construct nested records. Compatible dotted paths
merge, including across files in a workspace:

```gcsl
server.host = "localhost"
server.port = 8080
server.tls.enabled = true
```

A whole-field definition such as `server = {...}` closes that field to
further dotted extensions. Define the individual dotted leaves when you
want several files to contribute to a shared structure.

A spread starts from another record's visible fields. Inside an ordinary
record block, put defaults first and explicit overrides afterward:

```gcsl
let defaults = {host = "localhost"; port = 80}
let config = {
    ...defaults
    port = 8080
}
config.port
```

A spread copies only fields visible through its operand's static type.
It cannot replace an earlier explicit field inside an ordinary block.
Top-level declarations are resolved across the whole input and take
precedence over a spread of the same name regardless of textual order.

## Unions and narrowing

A union, such as `Int | String`, allows more than one shape. Use `is` to
test a value and `as` for a checked conversion that fails at runtime if
the value doesn't fit. In `if name is Type`, the branches see narrowed
copies of the named value. This does not perform general flow analysis of
later statements or dotted field paths.

Typed equality `==` compares operands of the same static type. Total
equality `===` compares complete runtime records, including fields hidden by
their shared static type. Both require the same static operand type. The
language's
restrictions on unions, functions, and futures are described in
`gcsl(5)` and [Inference Limits](/gcsl/language/inference-limits.md).

---

# Functions and Effects

_GCSL / The Language_

> Choose pure, impure, or memoized functions and understand captures.

A function's signature says what it accepts, what it returns, and whether
calling it can perform effects. This makes executable configuration
reviewable: a pure calculation cannot silently run a process or read a
file.

## Choosing a function form

Use `fn` for a private function and `def` for a function exposed as a
field. Both forms declare parameter and result types. A function
expression can also be assigned to a variable or passed to another
function.

The marker before the result type selects the effect:

| Marker | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `:` | Pure computation; no effects or awaiting |
| `->` | Impure computation; effects and awaiting are allowed |
| `->>` | Impure computation memoized by its arguments |

This private pure function accepts a named argument and a default:

```gcsl
fn scale(value: Int, factor: Int = 2): Int { value * factor }
scale(value = 21)
```

Positional and named arguments are evaluated in source order. A trailing
block supplies the last parameter, which is useful for functions that
accept a configuration record.

A default can be any expression. It can use the parameters before it and
anything visible where the function is written:

```gcsl
let suffix = ".o"
fn object(source: String, output: String = "{source}{suffix}"): String {
    output
}
main = object("main.c")
lib = object("lib.c", output = "lib.a")
```

A default is evaluated each time a call leaves its parameter out, after
the arguments that are written and before the function's body runs. It
has the function's effect, so a pure function's default cannot call an
impure function. A default cannot use its own parameter or a later one.
Only a call by the function's own name uses its defaults. A call through
a copy, such as `g` after `let g = object`, or through a parameter must
supply every argument.

After a named argument, a name on its own is shorthand for passing the
variable of that name to the parameter of that name. These two calls are
the same:

```gcsl
fn build(source: String, output: String, compiler: String): String {
    "{compiler} {source} -o {output}"
}
let compiler = "cc"
short = build("main.c", output = "main.o", compiler)
long = build("main.c", output = "main.o", compiler = compiler)
```

Before any named argument, a name on its own is an ordinary positional
argument.

## Generic functions

A function can work on values of any type. Name its type parameters in
angle brackets after its name, and use them as types in its signature and
body:

```gcsl
fn first<T>(items: List<T>, fallback: T): T {
    if len(items) > 0 { items[0] } else { fallback }
}
port = first([8080, 9090], 80)
name = first([], "none")
```

Each call works out the type parameters from its arguments: here `T` is
`Int` in the first call and `String` in the second. Inside the function, a
value of type `T` can be passed on, returned, or put in a list or record,
but nothing else, because `T` could be anything. A call where nothing binds
a type parameter, such as one that only returns a `List<T>`, is an error.

## Methods

A method is a function you call on a value with a dot. Write its
receiver in parentheses before its name:

```gcsl
fn (n: Int) twice(): Int { n * 2 }
fn (items: List<T>) first_or<T>(fallback: T): T {
    if len(items) > 0 { items[0] } else { fallback }
}
doubled = 21.twice()
port = [8080].first_or(80)
```

The value before the dot is the receiver. GCSL chooses the method by
the receiver's type, so methods with the same name can serve different
types. When more than one fits, the one for the more specific type is
used: a method for `List<Int>` is chosen over one for `List<T>` when
the receiver is a `List<Int>`. A method's other parameters, defaults and
effects work as a function's do.

A method has no name of its own: `twice` alone is not a variable, and
`x.twice` without parentheses reads a field. A record field and a method
with the same name are an error when both could apply.

Methods are declared at the top level of a file. A `fn` method is
visible only in its own file. A `def` method is also visible to the
other files of its directory and the directories below it, and to every
file that imports its module, whatever the form of the import. In the
REPL, methods are visible to later lines.

## Calling for effects

A call to an impure or memoized function can stand alone as a statement,
outside a pure function, to run it for its effects. Its value is
discarded, whatever it is, and anything it defers is awaited first, so no
effect is lost:

```gcsl
fn build(source: String) -> String {
    sh("make -C {source}")
    sh("make -C {source} install")
    = source
}
```

Any other value standing alone is an error, because discarding it would
do nothing: a pure expression has no effects to keep. Make it the block's
result with `= ...`, bind it with `let`, or remove it.

## Captures and recursion

Closures hold copies of the values they capture. Rebinding a variable
later doesn't change an existing closure. An inner function cannot rebind
an outer function's variable.

Top-level named functions are available regardless of textual order and
can refer to each other within the file. Workspace files still need an
acyclic dependency order: functions in two files cannot make those files
need each other. Put mutually dependent definitions together or extract
their shared dependencies.

## Memoization

A memoized function reuses a successful result for the same arguments in
the same execution context. The cache belongs to the closure, so two
closures created from one function expression have separate entries.
Memoization also accounts for supported ambient inputs such as a caller
path when the function reads it.

This is not a persistent build cache. External changes to files or a
command's environment do not invalidate a stored result. Use `->>` only
when reusing the first result within that run is correct. Cached keys and
results consume memory for the lifetime of their execution context.

Read [Deferred Work](/gcsl/language/deferred-work.md) before using futures in function
signatures; a bare type's present-or-future meaning depends on its
position and the function's effect.

---

# Attributes

_GCSL / The Language_

> Tell the program that runs a function how to run it.

An attribute goes before a function and says how to run it, for example
whether a build step may be cached or how long it may take. GCSL checks
every attribute. Most are read by the tool that runs the program, such as
a build system, and GCSL itself acts on its own two, `retry` and
`timeout`.

```gcsl
#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true}

#[hermetic]
fn compile(source: String) -> String { "compiled {source}" }

#[hermetic(false)] #[timeout(10m)]
fn deploy(target: String) -> String { "deployed {target}" }

result = deploy(compile("main.c"))
```

## Declaring an attribute

An attribute's arguments form a record, so you declare an attribute as a
record type marked `#[attribute]`. Each field is an argument, and a field
with a default is an argument you can leave out:

```gcsl
#[attribute] type device = {name: String, count: Int = 1}

#[device(name = "kvm")]
fn boot() -> String { "booted" }

booted = boot()
```

Arguments must be constants: literals, or lists and records of them. A
field may be an `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `String`, `Duration` or `Size`, or
a list or record of those. An attribute is declared, imported and named in
the same way as any other type, so a tool usually provides its attributes
in a module that you import.

## Using an attribute

Write `#[NAME]`, or `#[NAME(ARGUMENTS)]`, on the line before a function: a
`fn`, a `def`, or a definition made with a definition type. An attribute
with one field can take its argument by position, as in `#[timeout(10m)]`;
one with more fields takes its arguments by name. A misspelt attribute is
an error, so a mistake isn't silently ignored.

GCSL provides two attributes of its own, `retry` and `timeout`:

| Attribute | Argument | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `#[retry(times)]` | `times: Int` | If the call fails, run it again, up to this many more times. |
| `#[timeout(limit)]` | `limit: Duration` | Fail the call if it runs for longer than this. |

A retried call runs its whole body again with the same arguments, so any
effects it has happen again: use `retry` only where that's safe. Retrying a
pure function can't change its result, so GCSL warns about it. When a call
times out, a program it's running is stopped. A function's own timeout
counts as a failure that its `retry` can retry, and each attempt gets the
full time. A timeout of an enclosing call is never retried by a call inside
it.

```gcsl
#[retry(2)] #[timeout(30s)]
fn fetch(url: String) -> String { exec(["echo", "fetched {url}"]).stdout }

page = fetch("https://example.com")
```

## Attributes on a definition type

Attributes on a definition type apply to every definition made with it.
A definition's own attribute of the same name replaces the definition
type's:

```gcsl
#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true}

#[def_type] #[hermetic]
def check(name: String, f: fn(): Bool): fn(): Bool { f }

def check fast() { true }
#[hermetic(false)] def check online() { true }

results = [(check fast)()(), (check online)()()]
```

Here `fast` is hermetic and `online` is not.

---

# Context

_GCSL / The Language_

> Pass settings such as the target platform through every call without threading them by hand.

Some values matter deep inside a program without every function in
between caring about them: the platform you are building for, for
example, or the toolchains available. A *context* is a named value of
that kind. You declare it once, read it with `^`, and set it for a call
and everything that call does with `with`:

```gcsl
context arch: String = "x86_64"

fn compiler(): String { "gcc-{^arch}" }
fn build(): String { "built with {compiler()}" }

native = build()
cross = build() with { arch = "aarch64" }
```

`build` never mentions `arch`, yet `cross` is built with `gcc-aarch64`:
the `with` reaches every call made inside it.

## Declaring a context

`context NAME: TYPE = DEFAULT` declares a context and its default, at the
top level of a file. Every file of the workspace can read it without an
import, so a name is declared only once. A context can leave out its
default, `context toolchains: List<Toolchain>`; reading it then needs a
`with` that sets it, and reading it without one is an error.

A default is an expression, and it is evaluated where the context is
read, in the context of the read. So one context can default to another,
and follow it:

```gcsl
type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
context host_platform: Platform = {arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"}
context platform: Platform = ^host_platform

target = ^platform.arch with { host_platform = {arch = "aarch64"; os = "linux"} }
```

A default must be pure: it can't call an impure function. To use a value
that needs one, compute it first and bind it with `with`.

## Setting a context with `with`

`EXPR with { NAME = VALUE }` evaluates `EXPR` with the context `NAME`
set to `VALUE`. It applies to the call or field access just before it,
so `a + f() with {...}` sets the context for `f()` alone. It must be on
the line where the expression ends. To set a context for several
statements, use the block form, `with { NAME = VALUE } { ... }`.

A dotted name sets one field of a record context and keeps the others:

```gcsl
type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
context platform: Platform = {arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"}

fn triple(): String { "{^platform.arch}-{^platform.os}" }
riscv = triple() with { platform.arch = "riscv64" }
```

`with { arch }` is short for `with { arch = arch }`, setting the context
from a variable of the same name.

## Reading a context in a parameter

A parameter written `^NAME` takes its default from the context of that
name, so a caller can still pass a value explicitly. `fn f(^arch)` is the
same as `fn f(arch: String = ^arch)`, with the type taken from the
context's declaration:

```gcsl
context arch: String = "x86_64"
fn compiler(^arch): String { "gcc-{arch}" }

usual = compiler()
explicit = compiler(arch = "mips")
```

## Context and deferred work

A `lazy` keeps the context it was made in. Wherever it is later
awaited, it reads the values that were set where it was written. A
top-level field is computed when its file runs, so it reads the default
values.

Memoised functions stay correct: a memoised call's result is reused only
for a call made in a context that gives every context the call read the
same value.

---

# Deferred Work

_GCSL / The Language_

> Delay effects with lazy expressions and resolve them with await.

A future represents a calculation whose result is needed later. `lazy`
creates that calculation without running it. Awaiting it evaluates the
body and remembers the successful result. Futures are deferred work, not
concurrent tasks.

This program creates one future and then reads its result:

```gcsl
let answer = lazy 21 * 2
await answer
```

## Where work happens

A `lazy` body is an impure context, even when a pure function creates it.
That lets a pure function plan effects without performing them. A pure
function cannot await a future. Resolve the value in an impure caller
before passing it into a pure calculation that needs the value itself.

The checker inserts implicit awaits at uses that need a value's contents,
such as arithmetic or a required present argument. It applies the same
purity restriction to explicit and implicit awaits. A call to a pure
function never secretly starts deferred work inside that function.

## Writing contracts

Use explicit wrappers when the distinction is part of an interface:

| Type | Contract |
|---|---|
| `Present<Int>` | A present integer, equivalent to `Int` in an ordinary annotation |
| `Future<Int>` | Deferred work yielding an integer |
| `Int | Future<Int>` | Either a present or a deferred integer |

A present value does not satisfy an explicitly required `Future<T>`.
Nested future layers collapse: awaiting a future resolves it fully.
Awaiting an already present value is a silent no-op.

Bare types in impure parameters accept present or deferred values,
including bare positions inside records and lists. Pure parameters infer
whether their bodies need the value's contents. Impure and memoized
results infer their present-or-future shape from the body. Explicit
wrappers keep the contract you wrote.

## Avoiding surprising inference

A pure function with result `Int` requires a present result, even if it
only returns its argument. It does not preserve a future's shape
polymorphically. Recursive result inference also retains conservative
parts of the declared signature. Use `Present<T>` or an explicit future
contract where an API needs a stable distinction, and consult
[Inference Limits](/gcsl/language/inference-limits.md) when an inferred type is wider
than expected.

---

# Lists and Control Flow

_GCSL / The Language_

> Build lists, repeat expressions, and leave loops or functions.

Lists hold ordered values of one element type. GCSL's loops are
expressions, so a loop can collect results into a list or run only for its
effects. This avoids a separate collection-building language.

## Lists and ranges

`List<Int>` and `Int[]` name the same type. Indexes start at zero; a
negative or out-of-range index is a runtime error. A spread inserts
another list's elements. An empty list needs an expected element type:

```gcsl
let empty: List<Int> = []
let values = [...empty, 3, 8, 5]
values[1]
```

`0..3` produces `[0, 1, 2]`; `0..=3` includes `3`. Bounds are integers and
ranges run upward. A range used directly as a loop's iterable is counted
without first allocating the list of all its integers.

## Collecting and repeating

A loop used as a value collects each iteration's result. A guard skips
iterations that don't match:

```gcsl
let doubled = for n in [1, 2, 3] if n > 1 { n * 2 }
doubled
```

A loop used as a statement discards its results. Use `for condition` for
repetition while a condition holds, `for { ... }` without a condition,
or `do { ... } for condition` to test after the body. A header can contain
initialization and an update:

```gcsl
let values = for let i = 0; i < 3; i := i + 1 { i * 2 }
values
```

A plain header variable can be rebound by the header. Write `let mut` if
the body must also update it.

## Leaving a computation

`break` ends the enclosing loop and `continue` skips to its next
iteration. Either can supply a value to the collecting loop before
leaving that iteration. An unlabeled `return` returns from the enclosing
function or `lazy` body.

Labels identify an outer loop or a block. A labeled `return` leaves that
target with one value; it does not append to a loop's accumulated list.
Control flow cannot jump out through a function boundary. See `gcsl(5)`
for label syntax and the distinction between collected and returned
values.

---

# Inference Limits

_GCSL / The Language_

> Recognize supported inference and use explicit types at its boundaries.

GCSL infers local values and selected present-or-future positions. It does
not infer every relationship between types. Explicit annotations help you
state an intended interface when a program reaches one of these limits.

The following boundaries affect ordinary programs:

| Boundary | What to write |
|---|---|
| An empty list has no independent element type | Give it an annotation or an expected `List<T>` type |
| A pure function does not preserve arbitrary future shapes | Write the present or future contract explicitly |
| Recursive impure results can retain a deferred possibility | Use `Present<T>` when the result must be present |
| General type parameters and intersections are unsupported | Write concrete structural types |
| There is no `None` value or optional-type shorthand | Represent alternatives using supported union members |
| Narrowing recognizes a plain name tested directly by `if` | Bind a field to a name before testing it |
| Narrowing does not establish facts after an early return | Keep uses inside the narrowed branch |
| A narrowed mutable name is a branch-local copy | Don't try to update the original through that copy |
| `len` and `show` use special call rules | Call them directly or through supported aliases, rather than storing them as ordinary function values |

Expected types reach record fields, call arguments, and other supported
literal contexts. An unrelated sibling expression is not a general
constraint-solving mechanism. For example, annotate an empty list before
combining it with independently inferred values.

Warnings do not prevent execution. A union inferred from unlike branch
results can produce a warning while the program remains valid. Use
`gcsl check` to inspect the full set of errors and warnings, and
`gcsl run --dump-types FILE` when the inferred shape is unclear.

These are supported-language boundaries, not promises about a future
inference engine. The checker implementation and its regression tests
are the source for the behavior described here.

---

# Workspaces

_GCSL / Files and Workspaces_

> Understand directory scopes, imports, and checking across files.

A GCSL workspace is a directory tree rooted at `workspace.gcsl`. Files in
one directory share public declarations. Child directories inherit the
public declarations above them, so common types and helpers can live at
the root without repeated imports.

The marker is an ordinary GCSL source file. It can be empty or declare
shared values and functions. The disk loader finds the nearest enclosing
marker when you run a file, reads the workspace, and checks every source
before executing the selected file and its dependencies.

## Public and private names

Fields, `def` functions, and declared types contribute to a directory's
public scope. `let`, `let mut`, and `fn` bindings remain private to their
file. Dotted public paths merge when they define distinct leaves; a
conflicting leaf is an error unless an earlier declaration explicitly
permits shadowing.

Sibling and child directories are not automatically in scope. Imports
name paths from the workspace root, with `/` between directories, as the
file system does. `import lib/helpers` names `lib/helpers.gcsl` or a
directory of that path. Use `as` for a local alias, or import a named
member with `from`.

A directory or file name in a path is written as it is, dots and all, so
`import pkgs/net.busybox.busybox as busybox` names the directory
`pkgs/net.busybox.busybox`. An import without `as` is named after the
path's last part, so that part must be a plain name; give a name such as
`net.busybox.busybox` one with `as`.

## Boundaries and evaluation order

Hidden names and nested directories containing their own `workspace.gcsl`
are excluded from an enclosing workspace's source discovery. A nested
workspace is independent, not an importable child module of the outer
workspace. Symbolic-link directory traversal is not a mechanism for
extending the source tree.

Dependencies determine file order; path order breaks ties. A file runs
as a whole before a file that depends on it. Cyclic file dependencies are
errors, including cycles introduced through function bodies or imports.

A standalone file outside any workspace can import standard modules but
cannot import arbitrary neighboring source files. Create a workspace
marker when several files need to cooperate.

## Deliberate overrides

`#[shadowable]` marks a public declaration or import as replaceable by a
later declaration in the workspace's defined order. The implicit prelude
import is shadowable, so your own declarations can use its names.

Use this attribute for intentional defaults. It is not an escape from
all conflicts: an ordinary public leaf still cannot be defined twice.
Whole-value overrides replace the old value, while distinct dotted
leaves can merge. See `gcsl(5)` for ordering and import syntax.

---

# Organizing a Workspace

_GCSL / Files and Workspaces_

> Split shared declarations from an executable entry file.

You can share a type and helper across files by putting them in a
workspace. This example gives the entry file access to root declarations
without an import.

## Before you start

You need `gcsl` on your command search path and an empty writable directory.

## Create the workspace

1. Save these declarations as `workspace.gcsl`:

   ```gcsl
   type Component = {name: String, version: String}
   def describe(item: Component): String {
       "{item.name} {item.version}"
   }
   ```

2. Save the entry program as `main.gcsl` beside it:

   ```gcsl
   describe({name = "gcsl"; version = "0.1.0"})
   ```

3. From that directory, check the workspace and run the entry file:

   ```sh
   gcsl check
   gcsl run main.gcsl
   ```

## Check the result

The check reports `no problems in 2 files checked`. The run prints
`gcsl 0.1.0`. The helper is visible because `def` contributes to the
shared directory scope. Replacing `def` with `fn` makes it private to
`workspace.gcsl`, so the entry file cannot resolve it.

When adding a child library directory, import its public fields or
functions by its root-relative path. Keep independent projects behind
their own workspace markers rather than relying on hidden files to
separate their scopes.

---

# File and Process Effects

_GCSL / Files and Workspaces_

> Understand paths, process execution, and temporary-file lifetime.

GCSL provides standard functions for files and subprocesses. Their effects
use the host process's authority. Absolute paths and explicit command
arguments make those effects easier to inspect, but don't restrict what
a program can access.

## Locating files

Import `fileroot` to locate source directories and `path` to compose paths.
`fileroot.this` is the directory of the file containing the expression;
`fileroot.workspace` is the workspace root. `fileroot.caller` identifies
the source directory of the running function's caller.

`path.join`, `path.normalize`, `path.dirname`, and `path.basename` operate
lexically. They don't resolve symbolic links or prove that a path stays
inside a directory. Use them for path construction, not access control.

File operations under `fs` require absolute paths. The following program
writes and reads a temporary file, then removes its temporary directory
when the run ends:

```gcsl
import fs
import path
let directory = fs.temp_dir()
let file = path.join(directory, "message.txt")
fs.write(file, "GCSL")
fs.read(file)
```

Temporary directories last for the owning run, workspace, or REPL session.
Normal cleanup also runs after an evaluation error. Killing the process
can leave temporary directories behind. Atomic replacement by `fs.write`
protects readers from partial contents; it does not preserve a replaced
file's permissions or provide a durable transaction across several files.

## Running a process

`exec` accepts a list whose first element names the program. Later elements
are passed as arguments without shell interpretation. It returns a record
with the exit status and captured output. A nonzero exit status is data,
not a GCSL runtime error.

`sh` instead executes a string through `/bin/sh` and fails the GCSL run
when the command fails. Don't insert untrusted text into that string.
Prefer `exec` when values need to become arguments rather than shell code.

`exec_live` and `sh_live` also copy child output to GCSL's standard error
as lines arrive. They still capture output in memory. All these commands
inherit the host environment and working directory, and give the child
null standard input. A source-relative `fileroot` value does not change
a command's working directory.

Consult `gcsl-stdlib(3)` for exact function contracts and error behavior.

---

# Checking and Formatting Programs

_GCSL / Development Tools_

> Check types and apply the standard GCSL formatter.

You can check a GCSL program without running its effects, then format its
source consistently. The checker and formatter deliberately have different
scopes: checking needs workspace relationships; formatting only needs
source text that parses.

## Before you start

You need `gcsl` and an existing source file or workspace. Commit or save
your changes before rewriting files so you can review the formatting diff.

## Check and format

1. Check a file, including its enclosing workspace if it has one:

   ```sh
   gcsl check main.gcsl
   ```

2. Preview whether formatting would change that file:

   ```sh
   gcsl fmt --check main.gcsl
   ```

3. Apply the formatter and repeat the check:

   ```sh
   gcsl fmt main.gcsl
   gcsl fmt --check main.gcsl
   ```

## Check the result

The checker reports its summary on standard error. Warnings alone do not
make it fail. The final formatting check succeeds without listing the
file when it is already formatted.

A file with lexical or syntax errors is left untouched by the formatter;
fix those diagnostics and retry. Formatting does not require a successful
type check. It uses one language style rather than editor-specific indent
settings.

## Checking a tree

With no paths, both commands use the enclosing workspace and exclude
nested workspaces. An explicit directory makes them discover nested
workspaces too. However, `gcsl check DIRECTORY` checks workspaces, not
unattached `.gcsl` files under that directory. Pass each standalone file
explicitly when it also needs checking.

For automation, `gcsl check --message-format json main.gcsl` writes one
JSON diagnostic per line to standard output and keeps the summary on
standard error. See `gcsl(1)` for the schema and exit statuses.

---

# Configuring an Editor

_GCSL / Development Tools_

> Connect an editor to the bundled GCSL language server.

The GCSL language server supplies diagnostics and navigation from the same
checker that runs programs. You can use it in an editor that supports the
Language Server Protocol (LSP) over standard input and standard output.

## Before you start

You need the following:

- An editor with configurable LSP support.
- The `gcsl` executable on the editor's command search path.
- A GCSL file or workspace to open.

## Connect the server

1. Associate files ending in `.gcsl` with a GCSL language entry in your
   editor.
2. Set the server command to `gcsl` and its argument list to `["lsp"]`.
   Use standard input and standard output for transport.
3. Open a GCSL file. For a multifile project, open the workspace directory
   so the editor can supply file changes and watch notifications.

## Check the result

Hover over a declared name to see its type. Introduce a misspelled name and
confirm that the editor displays a diagnostic. When an unambiguous
spelling suggestion is available, a quick fix offers the replacement.

The server checks source but does not evaluate programs. It supports
completion, signature help, inlay hints, formatting, symbol search, and
references. Rename checks the proposed edits before returning them and
refuses changes that introduce errors or change name resolution. It does
not rename files, directories, or standard-library declarations.

## Incomplete source

The index retains useful information around many syntax errors. For an
unbalanced block, a query can use a repaired copy to answer navigation or
completion while diagnostics still describe the real text. Rename needs
the real source to be valid enough to verify and can be refused instead.

Editor syntax highlighting is a separate integration. The grammar and
queries in `tree-sitter-gcsl` are maintained in their own repository; they
are not required for the `gcsl lsp` process to check your code.

---

# Embedding GCSL

_GCSL / Embedding GCSL_

> Choose a language-library entry point and preserve its execution requirements.

The `gcsl` Rust crate exposes the language pipeline without requiring the
command-line program. You can evaluate one source, retain an interactive
session, or load a workspace supplied by your application.

## Choosing the boundary

The repository separates four responsibilities:

| Crate | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `gcsl` | Syntax, types, evaluation, formatting, and semantic indexing |
| `gcsl-disk` | Finding workspace roots and reading source trees |
| `gcsl-lsp` | Editor protocol, document state, and request handling |
| `gcsl-cli` | Command arguments, terminal interaction, and output |

`gcsl::run` evaluates a `SourceFile` as a standalone program. A `Session`
retains names and values across inputs. `workspace::Workspace` evaluates
selected files after a workspace check. The lower-level lexer, parser,
checker, and index are available when you only need a particular stage.
Consult the Rust API documentation for exact signatures and ownership.

## Stack and lifetime

Run the language pipeline on a thread with at least `gcsl::STACK_SIZE`
bytes of stack, as the CLI does. Its configured size is 64 MiB. Parsing,
checking, lowering, and evaluation share documented nesting limits; a host
thread's default stack is not a substitute for the required reservation.

Keep the source collection and diagnostic input numbering together.
Standard-library files participate in the source list, so diagnostics can
point into a helper as well as into user text. The library returns
warnings with successful results instead of printing them.

A workspace or session owns memoization state and temporary directories.
Dropping it performs normal temporary-directory cleanup. Do not return a
path to such a directory and then destroy its owner before a consumer has
used it.

## Effects are host effects

The standard library is embedded in the binary. Its process and file
operations still run with the embedding process's permissions and
resources. The library does not impose a wall-clock limit or turn pure
checking into an execution sandbox. A host that accepts untrusted programs
needs an external isolation and resource policy.

Use `gcsl-disk` when you want the CLI's workspace-discovery behavior.
Supply sources yourself when your host has a different storage model;
keep the language's dependency and scope rules intact.

---

# Definition Types and Metadata

_GCSL / Embedding GCSL_

> Build domain-specific declarations without changing the GCSL parser.

A definition type lets a host or library describe a family of named
functions. The language checks each definition against a shared signature,
while the host can discover and invoke definitions by name. This is the
mechanism used by integrations such as Pekit.

## A family of definitions

Mark a public function with `#[def_type]`. Its first parameter receives the
definition's path as a string; its second receives the definition function.
Further parameters become invocation arguments. This example wraps a
pure string-producing definition:

```gcsl
#[def_type]
def describe(name: String, body: fn(): String): String {
    "{name}: {body()}"
}
def describe dev.peios.gcsl() { "language tools" }
result = describe dev.peios.gcsl()
```

Declaring the definition does not execute its body. Calling its reference
does. Definitions live in the definition type's namespace, separate from
ordinary values of the same path. Their public paths merge through
workspace directories and imports like other public declarations.

## Definitions made with a variant

A definition type can take a variant of an [enum](/gcsl/language/records-and-types.md)
after the name, and the definition after that. Each definition then
names a variant, and each variant has its own definitions. When the
definition type is generic, the variant's payload type fills in its type
parameter, so each definition's body is checked against its own
variant's payload:

```gcsl
enum Tool extensible<{*: String}>
enum Tool::C<{cc: String}>
enum Tool::Rust<{rustc: String}>
#[def_type]
def Tool<T>(name: String, kind: Variant<Tool, T>, body: fn(): T): Tool {
    kind(body())
}
def Tool::C gcc() { cc = "gcc" }
def Tool::Rust gcc() { rustc = "rustc" }
c = Tool::C gcc()
rust = Tool::Rust gcc()
```

`Tool::C gcc` and `Tool::Rust gcc` are separate definitions. A reference
names the variant in the same way as the definition, and the definition
type receives the variant as its `kind` argument. Hosts don't invoke these
definitions by name: programs reach them through references, for example
in a list of registered toolchains.

## Metadata

Function types can carry metadata with `META:`, `META->`, or `META->>`.
These distinguish pure reads, impure reads, and memoized reads. A
`defmeta` declaration supplies the metadata of a definition in its
directory. Reading a field through the function reference reads that
metadata according to its effect.

Metadata is not a separate package-format contract. The definition type
chooses its shape, and the host integration owns what those fields mean.
Use the host's documentation for package or build semantics.

## Host invocation

A checked workspace exposes definition types and their definitions. Host
APIs can invoke a selected definition with named textual arguments parsed
against its declared parameter types. When a value the host gets back holds
a reference, such as `build = build busybox`, the host can ask which
definition it names and then invoke it, or read its metadata, by name. The
host can also invoke a definition in a [context](/gcsl/language/context.md) of its
choosing, such as the platform a build step runs on, by giving each
context a value of its declared type, for example one it got back from
an earlier run. Only that invocation sees those values: what each file
computes as it runs uses the declared defaults. The
`gcsl` executable itself exposes
`run`, `check`, `fmt`, and `lsp`; it does not add a general command for each
definition type.

Keep protocol or package policy in the integration repository. Keep
language syntax, type checking, and discovery semantics in GCSL. This
separation lets other hosts use the same language without inheriting
Pekit's packaging model.

---

# 100 The Language Pipeline

_GCSL / gcsl-internals_

> Follow source through checking and type-specialized evaluation.

GCSL is a tree-walking interpreter with a typed intermediate
representation. The stages are separate so a formatter or editor can stop
before execution while using the same syntax and type rules as the CLI.

The main pipeline is:

```text
SourceFile
    -> lexer and interned names/strings
    -> parser and arena-based syntax tree
    -> workspace resolution and type-check side tables
    -> type-specialized intermediate representation
    -> evaluation and a value with warnings
```

The lexer and parser can recover enough structure to report multiple
errors. A failed check is never lowered into executable code. Recovery
supports diagnostics and editor queries; it does not make a partially
valid program executable.

The checker resolves names and types expressions, recording captures,
slots, argument destinations, and required awaits in side tables.
Lowering makes those awaits explicit. Evaluation dispatches through
specialized operations whose types have already been checked, rather than
repeating the type system at each operation.

## 100.1 Workspace scheduling

The workspace scanner merges public field paths, resolves imports, and
orders source files by dependencies. Each public leaf has an owner and a
cell. Reading an interior record can require all its contributing owners.
A cycle is a diagnostic, not an order selected at runtime.

Top-level field scheduling differs from ordinary block order. It supports
forward references while ensuring dependencies are initialized before a
read. Shadowed declarations are still checked even when they do not
contribute a runtime value.

## 100.2 Sources

The principal implementation is in `crates/gcsl/src/lexer.rs`,
`parser.rs`, `workspace.rs`, `check.rs`, `lower.rs`, and `eval.rs`.
Each module documents its internal invariants. The implementation here is
not the separate Tree-sitter grammar used for highlighting.

---

# 200 Evaluation State

_GCSL / gcsl-internals_

> Understand values, futures, memo tables, and failure cleanup.

GCSL evaluation uses reference-counted values and explicit execution
contexts. A closure captures values; a frame holds the current call's
slots. This supports value semantics without exposing shared mutable
variables to programs.

## 200.1 Futures and memo tables

A future begins pending, becomes running during evaluation, and records a
successful result. Reentering running work is an error rather than an
infinite cycle through the same pending computation. A failed evaluation
does not become a cached success.

Memo tables belong to the execution context and are keyed by closure
identity and argument values, with supported ambient inputs included when
used. Keeping tables outside closures avoids introducing reference cycles
through cached keys and results. Successful entries for live closures
remain until their context ends; they are not an eviction-based cache.

## 200.2 Exits and cleanup

Runtime errors and language control flow share an unwinding path through
the evaluator. A loop handles its own `break` or `continue`; a function,
future, or labeled block handles a matching `return`. Intermediate
operations restore stack and evaluation state before propagating an exit.

A failed REPL input does not commit its new bindings or memo-table changes
to the session. Effects already performed outside the interpreter are not
rolled back. A file written or a subprocess started before an error still
requires the host's ordinary handling.

A workspace remembers the first runtime failure of each file. A later
request that needs that file receives the original diagnostic without
rerunning its effects or starting dependent files. Successful prior files
keep their values, and unrelated files can still run. Create a new
workspace object when you intentionally need to retry evaluation.

## 200.3 Resource limits

The parser's maximum expression depth is `gcsl::MAX_DEPTH`, 10,000 levels.
Runtime expressions, calls, and awaits share a nesting budget; calls cost
additional levels. Iterations reuse their body's depth rather than adding
one level per iteration. Tail calls are not eliminated.

These limits protect the recursive implementation, not total computation.
A program can still loop indefinitely, allocate large values, capture
large process output, or retain many memoized results. The embedding host
owns any execution timeout and memory limit.

The main implementation lives in `eval.rs`, `value.rs`, and `session.rs`.

---

# 300 Tooling State

_GCSL / gcsl-internals_

> Understand source discovery, editor snapshots, and formatter boundaries.

GCSL tooling shares language analysis while keeping disk access and editor
protocol handling outside the core. This prevents checking and formatting
from depending on an evaluator run.

## 300.1 Source discovery

`gcsl-disk` finds workspace markers and returns a source collection with
stable path and diagnostic mappings. It excludes hidden names and nested
workspace trees when reading one workspace. The CLI and language server
use this shared layer rather than implementing separate import rules.

A check of a directory discovers workspaces. A format of a directory
finds source files, including standalone files. Their traversal results
are therefore intentionally different.

## 300.2 Editor snapshots

`gcsl-lsp` overlays open document text on disk sources and asks a worker to
check the affected workspace. Queries use an up-to-date semantic index;
they can force a pending check before answering. Diagnostics continue to
refer to actual source even when a query temporarily repairs unmatched
braces to find usable names.

Rename computes proposed edits and checks their effect before offering
them. Standard-library, file, and directory identities are not editable
through that operation. Formatting returns edits from the language's
formatter rather than applying the editor's formatting preferences.

Only local `file:` document URIs with an empty or `localhost` authority
and an absolute decoded path map to disk files. Other URI forms are
checked as standalone documents, not treated as local workspace paths.

## 300.3 Formatter

The formatter works from tokens, comments, and parsed structure. It does
not need evaluation or successful type inference. Syntax errors prevent a
rewrite of that file. The CLI uses a temporary file and replacement so
readers don't observe a partially written formatted file.

The principal code is in `crates/gcsl-disk/src/lib.rs`,
`crates/gcsl-lsp/src/`, `crates/gcsl/src/index/`, and
`crates/gcsl/src/fmt.rs`. The CLI adapters live in
`crates/gcsl-cli/src/`.
